# research.local.env.example
#
# Copy this file to `research.local.env` (same directory, the repository root) and edit it.
# `research.local.env` is git-ignored: it names a path on ONE machine, which is exactly the
# thing this repository must not carry. This example is committed; the real file never is.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONFIGS -- the root of the lattice ensemble store
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every read under `research/` runs on:
#
#     "Entroptics lattice gauge-theory action-density ensembles (U(1), SU(2), SU(3))"
#     CC-BY-4.0, archived with its own DOI, cited separately from this repository.
#
# That is a SEPARATE DATA RELEASE -- about 25 GB of tarballs, roughly 49 GB once the collections
# are unpacked beside them. It is NOT part of this repository, it is not fetched by any script
# here, and it is far too large to be. You have to obtain it yourself, one of two ways:
#
#   * download and unpack the dataset record (see the "Data" section of the top-level README and
#     the paper's data-availability statement for the record); or
#   * regenerate it deterministically from the seed manifest with the pinned Entroptics release
#     -- generation is a function of (group, L, T, beta, seed, therm, method), and the store
#     ships the per-campaign updater / therm / seed map that fixes those.
#
# Point CONFIGS at the directory that holds the `configs_*` collection directories and the
# release manifest -- the root of the store, not one of the collections:
#
CONFIGS=/path/to/entroptics-lattice
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Precedence
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The environment variable of the same name is checked FIRST and wins over this file, so a single
# run can be pointed somewhere else without editing anything:
#
#     CONFIGS=/other/store python research/regen_all.py --only 8_7
#
# If neither is set there is NO fallback: the scripts refuse, and say so. That is deliberate --
# the previous fallback was an absolute path on the author's workstation, and on every other
# machine it made the reads find nothing while still exiting 0.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-script overrides (unchanged, still honoured)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A few certifications accept a narrower override for a single loose directory, which takes
# precedence over CONFIGS for that script only. These are for reading a hand-assembled subset
# (a pod scratch disk, one collection copied out), not for locating the store:
#
#   HOP    a single collection directory       (certify/apriori_A1.py, certify/gap_refinement_invariant.py)
#   HOP0   the baseline collection directory   (certify/gap_refinement_invariant.py)
#
# They are not set here; set them on the command line when you want them.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEAN_BUILD_* -- where `lake build` runs, and COMPUTE_* -- where heavy Python runs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Both are OPTIONAL and both default to "this machine", because a fresh clone must be able to
# reproduce everything without access to any particular box. They exist because both jobs are
# expensive enough that whoever has a machine for them should be able to say so in one place
# instead of in every caller.
#
# `research/code/lean_build.py` holds the Lean decision. A remote build ships the Lean SOURCES and
# compiles them there, so what is built is what is in this tree; it never ships or deletes
# `.lake`, which holds the Mathlib build the remote host exists for and is measured in gigabytes.
#
#   LEAN_BUILD_HOST=user@host            # ssh destination; absent, builds are local
#   LEAN_BUILD_PORT=22                   # optional
#   LEAN_BUILD_KEY=~/.ssh/some_key       # optional
#   LEAN_BUILD_DIR=massgap-lean          # remote Lean checkout, relative to $HOME
#   LEAN_BUILD_PATH=$HOME/.elan/bin      # optional, prepended to the remote PATH
#
# `research/code/remote_run.py` holds the Python decision -- certificates, reseeded sweeps, figure
# regeneration. A remote run ships the Python SOURCES and runs them there. It does NOT ship the
# ensemble store, so a script that READS the store runs locally regardless (`--needs-store`);
# a script that generates its own data runs anywhere.
#
#   COMPUTE_HOST=user@host                       # ssh destination; absent, runs are local
#   COMPUTE_PORT=22                              # optional
#   COMPUTE_KEY=~/.ssh/some_key                  # optional
#   COMPUTE_DIR=research                         # remote working tree, relative to $HOME
#   COMPUTE_PY=$HOME/research-venv/bin/python    # the interpreter there, holding requirements.txt
#
# SEPARATE KEYS ON PURPOSE. The two roles may well be the same machine, but they are different
# questions -- a Lean builder wants a warm Mathlib, a compute host wants cores -- and reading one
# set of keys for both would make moving the reads move the build with them. There is no fallback
# from one to the other.
#
#     python research/code/remote_run.py --describe     # where would a run go?
#     python research/code/remote_run.py --drift        # sources stranded on the remote

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ZENODO_TOKEN -- only needed to deposit the data record, not to read anything
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `research/code/zenodo_deposit.py` uploads the store to a Zenodo DRAFT deposition. It needs a
# personal access token with scopes `deposit:write` and `deposit:actions`, created at
#
#     https://zenodo.org/account/settings/applications/tokens/new/
#
# Sandbox and production are SEPARATE SITES with separate accounts and separate tokens; a sandbox
# token returns 401 against the production API and vice versa. Rehearse on the sandbox
# (https://sandbox.zenodo.org) before depositing for real -- publishing a record mints a permanent
# DOI and fixes the public record date.
#
# The token belongs here for the same reason CONFIGS does: this file is git-ignored, and a
# credential must never reach the repository. Nothing reads it at import, nothing logs it, and it
# is not written into the staged manifest. Leave it unset unless you are depositing:
#
# ZENODO_TOKEN=
#
# The environment variable of the same name wins over this file, as with CONFIGS:
#
#     ZENODO_TOKEN=... python research/code/zenodo_deposit.py upload --api sandbox
